# Database

The server runs on PostgreSQL. You create the logins, migrate once per release, and serve with either one login (local and small self-hosted deployments) or two (recommended for production). Wyrd never creates roles or grants privileges; it only checks that the logins you supply have the posture below.

## Logins

Every Wyrd table forces row-level security. Tenant connections bind one tenant per transaction, and the `tenant_isolation` policy admits only that tenant's rows. Platform connections mark themselves as operator sessions, and the `operator_access` policy admits them to every row, but only for the login that owns Wyrd's objects: the login that ran `wyrd-server migrate`.

| Login | `WYRD_DATABASE_URL` | `WYRD_PLATFORM_DATABASE_URL` | Privileges you grant |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Platform | One-login mode | Two-login mode | Owns the database (or holds `CREATE` on it), so `migrate` creates and owns the `platform`, `wyrd`, `vala`, and `iceberg_catalog` schemas. |
| Tenant | Two-login mode | — | `USAGE` on `platform`, `wyrd`, and `vala`; `SELECT`, `INSERT`, `UPDATE`, `DELETE` on tables and `USAGE`, `SELECT` on sequences in `wyrd` and `vala`. Nothing in `iceberg_catalog`. |

Neither login may be a superuser or hold `BYPASSRLS`; Postgres exempts those from every policy, so boot refuses them. Neither needs `CREATEDB` or `CREATEROLE`.

One login is the simplest setup: create an ordinary login that owns the database.

```sql
CREATE ROLE wyrd LOGIN PASSWORD '…';
CREATE DATABASE wyrd OWNER wyrd;
```

Two logins keep a compromised serving process confined to row-level security: the tenant login does not own Wyrd's objects, so the operator policy never applies to it. As a cluster administrator, create both logins and the database, then grant the tenant login its privileges before you migrate:

```sql
CREATE ROLE wyrd_platform LOGIN PASSWORD '…';
CREATE ROLE wyrd_tenant LOGIN PASSWORD '…';
CREATE DATABASE wyrd OWNER wyrd_platform;
\connect wyrd
CREATE SCHEMA platform AUTHORIZATION wyrd_platform;
CREATE SCHEMA wyrd AUTHORIZATION wyrd_platform;
CREATE SCHEMA vala AUTHORIZATION wyrd_platform;
GRANT USAGE ON SCHEMA platform, wyrd, vala TO wyrd_tenant;
ALTER DEFAULT PRIVILEGES FOR ROLE wyrd_platform IN SCHEMA wyrd, vala
    GRANT SELECT, INSERT, UPDATE, DELETE ON TABLES TO wyrd_tenant;
ALTER DEFAULT PRIVILEGES FOR ROLE wyrd_platform IN SCHEMA wyrd, vala
    GRANT USAGE, SELECT ON SEQUENCES TO wyrd_tenant;
```

The login names are yours to choose. Grant the tenant login nothing beyond this list: boot refuses a separate tenant login that owns, or is a member of the owner of, a Wyrd table, holds `TRUNCATE`, `REFERENCES`, or `TRIGGER` on one, or holds any privilege on a table without row-level security.

## Migrations

Migrations are a separate one-off step, not part of server boot. Run it with the same URLs you serve with before starting or upgrading servers; it connects as the platform login:

```bash
WYRD_DATABASE_URL="postgresql://wyrd_tenant:tenant-pw@db.internal:5432/wyrd" \
WYRD_PLATFORM_DATABASE_URL="postgresql://wyrd_platform:platform-pw@db.internal:5432/wyrd" \
  wyrd-server migrate
```

It applies every Wyrd and Vala migration, validates the result, and exits. It is idempotent, so rerunning it after a partial failure is safe.

### When a migration fails

Take and verify a database backup before every upgrade; that verified backup is your recovery point. If `wyrd-server migrate` exits nonzero:

1. Stop the rollout. Servers that are still running keep serving, and new servers refuse to become ready against the incomplete schema.
2. Fix the cause the error names (permissions, disk, connectivity, or a conflicting object) and rerun `wyrd-server migrate`. Completed migrations are skipped and the failed one is retried.
3. If the database cannot be repaired, restore the verified backup and rerun the migration.

Wyrd has no down migrations and never restores automatically.

## Serving

`WYRD_DATABASE_URL` is the only required URL. `WYRD_PLATFORM_DATABASE_URL` is optional and defaults to it.

```bash
# Production: two logins.
export WYRD_DATABASE_URL="postgresql://wyrd_tenant:tenant-pw@db.internal:5432/wyrd"
export WYRD_PLATFORM_DATABASE_URL="postgresql://wyrd_platform:platform-pw@db.internal:5432/wyrd"
```

The server never runs DDL. Before it reports ready it checks that every migration it embeds is applied with a matching checksum, that every Wyrd table forces row-level security with exactly its expected policies, that the platform login owns Wyrd's objects, and that neither login is a superuser or holds `BYPASSRLS`. With two logins it also checks the tenant login against the list above. If any check fails, boot stops with `WYRD_SQL_503_SCHEMA_NOT_READY` naming the cause.

### Pool tuning

Pool settings are covered in [Configuration](/self-hosting/configuration/): `WYRD_PG_POOL_MAX` (default 32) and `WYRD_PG_POOL_ACQUIRE_MS` (default 5000). Size your cluster's `max_connections` above the sum of the tenant, Vala, and platform pools across replicas.

